   "decision": "Option 3, <strong>subject to a ministry ruling</strong> (DEP-10). Until then, clients can choose office pickup as a payment channel, but it is never used as the check.",
   "authority": "ADM Policy and Program Design.",
   "html": "<p><strong>Conflicting Requirements</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Requirement A</strong>: <a class=\"rid\" href=\"%BASE%/requirements/functional#fr-086\">FR-086</a>, <a class=\"rid\" href=\"%BASE%/requirements/business#br-009\">BR-009</a>. Outcomes do not depend on channel, and electronic payment is available.</li><li><strong>Requirement B</strong>: OB-SU-61. Hardship cheques are sent to the local office so that eligibility is re-checked every month.</li></ul><p><strong>Stakeholders Involved</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Head of Service Delivery Operations and clients</strong>: reach and dignity; clients should not have to visit an office every month to get paid.</li><li><strong>Policy authority and compliance</strong>: hardship eligibility re-checked every month.</li></ul><p><strong>Nature of Conflict</strong>:</p><ul><li>One mechanism meets two requirements. Paying electronically removes the monthly check <span class=\"cite\">PRIN-C8</span>.</li></ul><p><strong>Trade-off Analysis</strong>:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Option</th><th scope=\"col\">Pros</th><th scope=\"col\">Cons</th><th scope=\"col\">Impact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Option 1</strong>: Keep sending cheques to the office</td><td>✅ Check preserved</td><td>❌ Clients must travel to an office to be paid</td><td>Clients bear the cost</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Option 2</strong>: Pay electronically and drop the check</td><td>✅ Better reach</td><td>❌ Removes a policy control</td><td>Compliance bears the risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Option 3</strong>: Separate the two. A monthly re-check <a class=\"g\" href=\"%BASE%/glossary#work-item\">work item</a> that applies whatever the channel (<a class=\"rid\" href=\"%BASE%/requirements/functional#fr-040\">FR-040</a>), with the client choosing how to be paid</td><td>✅ Both requirements met</td><td>❌ Needs a policy ruling on what counts as a re-check</td><td>Both met if policy agrees</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Resolution Strategy</strong>: INNOVATE</p><p><strong>Decision</strong>: Option 3, <strong>subject to a ministry ruling</strong> (DEP-10). Until then, clients can choose office pickup as a payment channel, but it is never used as the check.</p><p><strong>Rationale</strong>: <a class=\"rid\" href=\"%BASE%/parameters#n15\">N15</a> has exposed two requirements met by one mechanism, which nobody saw until it was written down (PRIN <a class=\"rid\" href=\"%BASE%/parameters#n15\">N15</a>).</p><p><strong>Decision Authority</strong>: ADM Policy and Program Design.</p><p><strong>Impact on Requirements</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Modified</strong>: <a class=\"rid\" href=\"%BASE%/requirements/functional#fr-040\">FR-040</a> makes the monthly re-check a work item whatever the channel.</li></ul><p><strong>Stakeholder Management</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Compliance (loses the incidental check)</strong>: a stated re-check that can be audited replaces an unstated one.</li></ul><p><strong>Future Consideration</strong>:</p><ul><li>The same pattern may exist elsewhere. Phase D should look for controls that depend on a channel.</li></ul>"
